    "id": "polymarket_event_179510",
    "title": "How many inches of snow in NYC this weekend? (Jan 24-26)",
    "background": "No background available.",
    "resolution_criteria": "This market will resolve according to the total snowfall in Central Park, New York City for the 3-day weekend of January 24 through January 26 (inclusive) according to the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration.\n\nIf the recorded data falls exactly between two brackets, this market will resolve to the higher bracket.\n\nThe resolution source for this market will be NOAA, specifically the figures for January 24, January 25, and January 26 under \"New Snow (IN)\" when the \"Daily data for a month\" for \"NY-Central Park Area\" is selected at the https://www.weather.gov/wrh/climate?wfo=okx link once that figure is finalized for the specified days. \n\nIf no data is available by February 28, 2026, 11:59PM ET, this market will resolve to the lowest bracket.",
    "outcome": "10-12",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nThe New York City area received 10-12 inches of snow over the weekend of January 24-26, 2026, driven by a powerful winter storm named Winter Storm Fern. A disrupted polar vortex allowed Arctic air to plunge southward, combining with a moisture-rich system moving up the coast. Initial models were split, causing uncertainty and market fluctuations, but the storm ultimately intensified rapidly, leading to heavy snowfall bands directly over the city. By January 26, predictions solidified around the 10-12 inch outcome as the storm delivered double-digit totals across the Tri-State area.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **Mid-January 2026**: A stratospheric warming event disrupted the polar vortex, allowing frigid Arctic air to spill into the U.S., setting the stage for major winter weather ([art_science_20260115_011_72f8cab1]).\n- **January 20-23, 2026**: Forecast models began tracking a potential major snowstorm for the Northeast. However, models clashed on the exact track and snowfall amounts, leading to uncertainty ([art_general_20260120_008_d5c71862], [art_general_20260123_007_00ae9bfe]).\n- **January 24, 2026**: Forecasts still showed potential for heavy snow mixed with sleet, keeping the outcome uncertain and causing a significant dip in prediction markets ([art_general_20260124_005_16df3ddc]).\n- **January 25, 2026**: Winter Storm Fern intensified significantly. Heavy snow bands set up over NYC, and Central Park officially recorded 11.4 inches of snow, breaking records for the date ([art_general_20260125_005_1230bfc3], [art_general_20260125_002_23223986]).\n- **January 26, 2026**: The storm moved out, and official totals confirmed the 10-12 inch range, causing prediction markets to flip definitively to the correct outcome ([art_general_20260126_002_40e4ad13], [art_general_20260126_006_d0321119]).\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe root cause of the heavy snowfall was a disrupted polar vortex that brought deep Arctic cold into the Northeast ([art_science_20260115_011_72f8cab1]). This cold air interacted with a moisture-laden low-pressure system moving up the East Coast, causing rapid intensification (cyclogenesis). The proximate trigger for the specific 10-12 inch total was the storm's track, which positioned heavy snowfall bands directly over New York City on January 25, leading to snowfall rates of up to 1-3 inches per hour ([art_general_20260125_005_1230bfc3]). The resulting double-digit totals confirmed the 10-12 inch prediction by January 26 ([art_general_20260126_002_40e4ad13]).\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nEarly in the forecast period, there was a significant possibility of the storm tracking closer to the coast or slightly inland, which would have brought warmer air and mixed precipitation (sleet/rain) to the city, drastically reducing snow totals. This was evident in the model clashes around January 23-24 ([art_general_20260123_007_00ae9bfe], [art_general_20260124_005_16df3ddc]). However, the storm ultimately tracked just far enough offshore to keep NYC in the cold sector for entirely frozen precipitation.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: Disruption of the polar vortex and Arctic air outbreak.\n  - Date: Mid-January 2026\n  - Why it matters causally: Provided the necessary cold air for significant snowfall.\n  - Source: [art_science_20260115_011_72f8cab1]\n- E2: Divergent forecast models creating uncertainty.\n  - Date: Jan 23-24, 2026\n  - Why it matters causally: Caused fluctuations in prediction markets.\n  - Source: [art_general_20260123_007_00ae9bfe]\n- E3: Winter Storm Fern drops heavy snow on NYC.\n  - Date: Jan 25, 2026\n  - Why it matters causally: Delivered the actual 10-12 inches of snow.\n  - Source: [art_general_20260125_005_1230bfc3]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Polar vortex disruption brought cold air | [art_science_20260115_011_72f8cab1] | Jan 15, 2026 | 0.9 | Sets up the winter weather pattern. |\n| Model disagreement on snow totals | [art_general_20260123_007_00ae9bfe] | Jan 23, 2026 | 0.9 | Explains market fluctuations. |\n| NYC receives double-digit snow | [art_general_20260125_005_1230bfc3], [art_general_20260126_002_40e4ad13] | Jan 25-26, 2026 | 1.0 | Confirms the ground truth outcome. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nThe main uncertainty leading up to the event was the storm's precise track. A slight shift west would have introduced sleet or rain, resulting in <4 or 4-6 inches (Option 2 or 3). A slower-moving storm or more intense banding could have pushed totals into the 12-14 or 14+ inch ranges (Option 6 or 7). The eventual track perfectly threaded the needle for the 10-12 inch outcome.\n",
