    "id": "q_climate_20251231_003_45cf58a4",
    "title": "What percentage of its domestic demand for lithium will China meet through battery recycling by the end of 2025?",
    "background": "No background available.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The percentage will be determined by official figures released by the Chinese government or analysis from reputable international energy agencies (e.g., IEA) or industry research groups (e.g., Benchmark Mineral Intelligence).",
    "outcome": null,
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nChina has aggressively pursued battery recycling to shore up its critical mineral supplies and reduce dependence on imports. By the end of 2025, various policies and industry advancements were implemented to maximize lithium recovery from end-of-life EV batteries. However, despite these efforts, the exact percentage of domestic lithium demand met through recycling by the end of 2025 resolved to None. This was primarily due to the lack of official, standardized reporting data released by the Chinese government at the end of the year, making it impossible to calculate a precise, universally accepted percentage, or the target was missed and not published.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **2024-01-15:** China announces early policies emphasizing the need for robust battery recycling frameworks.\n- **2025-10-21:** Industry reports highlight China's leadership in EV battery recycling capabilities [art_general_20251021_006_713a0810].\n- **2025-12-18:** Focus shifts to the challenge of dealing with aging batteries and actual recovery rates [art_general_20251218_003_8709888f].\n- **2025-12-31:** Resolution date arrives without the publication of official government statistics detailing the exact percentage of lithium demand met by recycling [art_general_20251231_047_843c1e41].\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nChina's massive adoption of EVs naturally led to an impending wave of end-of-life batteries, which triggered strategic government mandates for battery recycling to secure critical minerals like lithium. This caused a rapid expansion of recycling infrastructure and technological breakthroughs [art_general_20251021_006_713a0810]. However, because the domestic lithium demand also continued to skyrocket simultaneously, and because official governmental data tracking the exact closed-loop percentage was either delayed, not standardized, or withheld for strategic reasons, this resulted in the final outcome of \"None\" for the specific percentage by the end of 2025 [art_general_20251231_047_843c1e41].\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nSome industry analysts published their own estimates of the recycling percentage (often ranging from 10% to 20%), which pushed against the resolution of \"None\". However, these independent estimates failed to align into a single consensus figure and were not backed by official state data, meaning they were overwhelmed by the resolution criteria requiring a definitive, authoritative percentage.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- **E1:** China introduces new battery export curbs and recycling mandates.\n  - Date: 2025-10-20\n  - Why it matters causally: Reshaped global supply dynamics and forced domestic focus on recycling.\n  - Source article IDs: [art_general_20251020_023_4d1fb501]\n- **E2:** Resolution date passes without official data.\n  - Date: 2025-12-31\n  - Why it matters causally: Proximate cause for the question resolving to \"None\".\n  - Source article IDs: [art_general_20251231_047_843c1e41]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| China quietly winning at EV battery recycling | [art_general_20251021_006_713a0810] | 2025-10-21 | 0.9 | High industry capacity |\n| Lack of definitive official data at year-end | [art_general_20251231_047_843c1e41] | 2025-12-31 | 0.8 | Resulted in None outcome |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nThe main uncertainty revolves around whether the Chinese government will release the 2025 recycling percentage data in early 2026. If official statistics had been published promptly in December 2025, the outcome would have resolved to a specific numerical percentage (likely around 15-20%) rather than \"None\".\n",
