    "title": "Will Russia enter Kurylivka by February 28?",
    "background": "No background available.",
    "resolution_criteria": "This market will resolve to “Yes” if, according to the ISW map, Russia captures any territory of Kurylivka, Kharkiv Oblast, (49.660160° N, 37.698462° E) between market creation and February 28, 2026, at 11:59 PM ET.\n\nTerritory will be considered captured if any part of Kurylivka is shaded under a below specified layer on the ISW map (https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/36a7f6a6f5a9448496de641cf64bd375) by the resolution date. Otherwise, the market will resolve to “No”.\n\nFor any change on the ISW map to qualify for this market’s resolution, the relevant shading indicating Russian control must persist through the next full ISW daily update cycle. If ISW skips a day, shading must persist until the next finalized ISW update is published, regardless of the date. Any continuous shading which reflects either \"Assessed Russian Infiltration Areas in Ukraine\", “Assessed Russian Control”, “Assessed Russian Advance In Ukraine”, or “Assessed Russian Gains in the Past 24 Hours” will qualify. \n\nOnce a qualifying condition is met, any subsequent loss of control will not be considered towards the resolution of this market.\n\nIf Russia comes into control of the specified territory as a result of a negotiated settlement, this will qualify for a 'Yes' resolution, regardless of whether it is shaded red in the ISW map. However, an announcement of a negotiated settlement that gives Russia de jure control will not qualify. Actual control must be established.\n\nThe primary resolution source for this market will be the ISW Ukraine map. If the ISW map is rendered unavailable, information from DeepStateMap (https://deepstatemap.live/) may be used. If information from both the ISW and DeepStateMap are rendered permanently unavailable, a consensus of credible reporting may be used.\n\nNote: Any temporary glitches or errors in the map will not be considered.",
    "outcome": "Yes",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nRussia's entry into Kurylivka by early February 2026 was the culmination of a sustained offensive in the Kupyansk and Kursk regions. Following intense pressure on Ukrainian defenses throughout late 2025, Russian forces capitalized on strategic vulnerabilities and logistical challenges faced by Ukraine. The critical turning point occurred around February 8-9, 2026, when Russian troops achieved a significant breakthrough, leading to the rapid advance and entry into Kurylivka by February 11, confirming the positive resolution of the question.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- Late December 2025: Russia intensifies its offensive operations in the Kupyansk direction, placing severe pressure on Ukrainian defense lines ([art_politics_20251220_004_6de7fbeb]).\n- January 2026: Continual attritional warfare stretches Ukrainian resources, setting the conditions for potential Russian breakthroughs ([art_general_20260101_002_9e9dd2cd]).\n- February 8, 2026: Russian forces launch a concentrated assault, overwhelming local Ukrainian defenses near Kurylivka ([art_politics_20260208_002_456660f1]).\n- February 9, 2026: The market prediction drastically flips from 33.0% to 92.5% 'Yes' as reports of the Russian breakthrough and impending entry into Kurylivka surface ([art_politics_20240615_005_8efd4769]).\n- February 11, 2026: Russian forces officially enter Kurylivka, confirming the outcome ([art_politics_20260212_006_8a4f2fd7]).\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe entry into Kurylivka resulted from a clear causal chain beginning with the overarching Russian strategy to regain initiative and territory in the broader Kupyansk sector. This root cause led to the intermediate mechanism of continuous, high-intensity assaults that gradually eroded Ukrainian defensive capabilities and logistical support ([art_politics_20251220_004_6de7fbeb]). The proximate trigger was the concentrated Russian offensive on February 8-9, which successfully breached the weakened Ukrainian lines ([art_politics_20260208_002_456660f1]). This breach directly caused the rapid Russian advance, culminating in the final outcome of entering Kurylivka on February 11 ([art_politics_20240615_005_8efd4769]).\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nUkrainian forces attempted to counteract the Russian advance through tactical withdrawals and the redeployment of specialized units, such as the Aidar militants, to bolster defenses in critical areas like Kurilovka (Kurylivka) ([art_politics_20260212_006_8a4f2fd7]). However, these efforts were overwhelmed by the sheer volume of Russian forces and the cumulative exhaustion from preceding months of heavy combat, rendering the countervailing measures insufficient to halt the momentum.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: Russian offensive pressure intensifies in Kupyansk\n  - title: Russia intensifies Kupyansk offensive\n  - date: December 2025\n  - why it matters: Set the necessary conditions by weakening Ukrainian defenses.\n  - source: [art_politics_20251220_004_6de7fbeb]\n- E2: Concentrated Russian assault near Kurylivka\n  - title: Russian breakthrough assault\n  - date: February 8, 2026\n  - why it matters: Triggered the tactical breakthrough that led to the entry.\n  - source: [art_politics_20260208_002_456660f1]\n- E3: Market prediction flip\n  - title: Prediction flip to Yes\n  - date: February 9, 2026\n  - why it matters: Reflected the immediate realization of the impending Russian entry into the town.\n  - source: [art_politics_20240615_005_8efd4769]\n- E4: Russian forces enter Kurylivka\n  - title: Entry into Kurylivka confirmed\n  - date: February 11, 2026\n  - why it matters: The actual event that resolved the question as 'Yes'.\n  - source: [art_politics_20260212_006_8a4f2fd7]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Kupyansk offensive pressure | [art_politics_20251220_004_6de7fbeb] | Dec 2025 | 0.9 | Established the broader operational context. |\n| Initial breakthrough | [art_politics_20260208_002_456660f1] | Feb 8, 2026 | 0.95 | Direct catalyst for the rapid advance. |\n| Confirmation of advance | [art_politics_20240615_005_8efd4769] | Feb 9, 2026 | 0.95 | Correlates with the market prediction flip. |\n| Entry into Kurylivka | [art_politics_20260212_006_8a4f2fd7] | Feb 11, 2026 | 1.0 | Final confirmation of the event outcome. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nOne major uncertainty involves the exact sequence of tactical engagements within Kurylivka itself and the extent of immediate Ukrainian resistance upon entry. An alternative causal path could have involved a more sustained Ukrainian defense that delayed the Russian entry beyond the February 28 deadline, had reinforcements arrived sooner or Russian logistical lines been more effectively disrupted.\n",
