    "id": "q_health_20251219_017_9a8f52d5",
    "title": "What will be the acquisition price of Amicus Therapeutics by BioMarin in the deal announced in December 2025?",
    "background": "No background available.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The acquisition price reported in the official announcement from BioMarin or Amicus Therapeutics, or by a reputable financial news source.",
    "outcome": 4800000000,
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nBioMarin Pharmaceutical acquired Amicus Therapeutics for $4.8 billion in December 2025. The transaction was driven by BioMarin's strategic need to expand its leadership in rare diseases and diversify its revenue streams. By acquiring Amicus, BioMarin gained established commercial assets, specifically Galafold for Fabry disease and Pombiliti + Opfolda for Pompe disease, which are expected to stabilize and accelerate its cash flows. The deal, priced at $14.50 per share in cash, was announced in late December 2025.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- Prior to December 2025: BioMarin searches for strategic acquisitions to bolster its rare disease portfolio and offset internal pipeline adjustments.\n- 2025-12-19: BioMarin and Amicus Therapeutics announce a definitive agreement for BioMarin to acquire Amicus for $4.8 billion at $14.50 per share in cash [art_finance_20251219_007_9af517c2].\n- 2025-12-22: Analysts raise price targets on BioMarin following the acquisition announcement, viewing the addition of Amicus's assets as a strong strategic fit [art_health_20251222_002_dc7480a6].\n- 2025-12-22: BioMarin drops a genetic liver disease asset from its pipeline, reallocating focus following the Amicus acquisition [art_health_20251222_004_9dc31e43].\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe acquisition of Amicus Therapeutics by BioMarin was primarily caused by BioMarin's strategic imperative to cement its position in the rare disease market [art_finance_20251219_007_9af517c2]. This broader strategic goal led to a search for targets with complementary, de-risked commercial assets. Amicus Therapeutics, possessing successful commercial therapies like Galafold and the Pombiliti + Opfolda combination, presented an ideal target. The availability of these assets triggered the initiation of acquisition talks. Ultimately, the synergy between BioMarin's commercial infrastructure and Amicus's product portfolio resulted in the final agreement for a $4.8 billion all-cash acquisition, because the financial terms ($14.50 per share) provided sufficient premium for Amicus shareholders while offering BioMarin immediate revenue diversification and accelerated growth [art_health_20251222_002_dc7480a6].\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nSome countervailing factors may have included the high cost of the acquisition and the integration risks associated with merging two significant biotech entities. Additionally, BioMarin had to balance the acquisition with its existing internal R&D pipeline, leading to the difficult decision to discard an internal genetic disease asset to rationalize its portfolio [art_health_20251222_004_9dc31e43]. However, these factors were overwhelmed by the immediate revenue stabilization and long-term growth potential provided by Amicus's commercialized products.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: BioMarin announces definitive agreement to acquire Amicus Therapeutics for $4.8 billion\n  - date: 2025-12-19\n  - why it matters causally: This is the primary resolution event that establishes the acquisition price and finalizes the deal.\n  - source article IDs: [art_finance_20251219_007_9af517c2], [art_health_20251222_002_dc7480a6]\n- E2: BioMarin drops internal genetic disease candidate\n  - date: 2025-12-22\n  - why it matters causally: Demonstrates the immediate strategic realignment and portfolio rationalization following the major acquisition.\n  - source article IDs: [art_health_20251222_004_9dc31e43]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| BioMarin to acquire Amicus for $4.8 billion | [art_finance_20251219_007_9af517c2], [art_health_20251222_002_dc7480a6] | 2025-12-19 | 1.0 | Official announcement and press coverage confirm the price and terms. |\n| Deal adds Galafold and Pombiliti + Opfolda to BioMarin portfolio | [art_finance_20251219_007_9af517c2] | 2025-12-19 | 1.0 | Primary strategic rationale cited by the companies. |\n| BioMarin drops genetic disease asset post-acquisition | [art_health_20251222_004_9dc31e43] | 2025-12-22 | 0.9 | Consequence of the acquisition and portfolio prioritization. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nThe main uncertainty during the negotiation phase was likely the final premium to be paid for Amicus shares. Alternative paths could have seen Amicus acquired by another large pharmaceutical company looking to expand its rare disease footprint, or Amicus remaining independent and relying on organic growth from its recently approved therapies. Another uncertainty is the regulatory approval of the merger, though the complementary nature of the portfolios likely minimizes antitrust concerns.\n",
