    "id": "polymarket_event_281072",
    "title": "Israel military action against Gaza on...?",
    "background": "No background available.",
    "resolution_criteria": "This market will resolve to \"Yes\" if Israel initiates a drone, missile, or air strike on Gaza soil on the listed date Israel Standard Time (IST). Otherwise, this market will resolve to \"No\".\n\nFor the purposes of this market, a qualifying \"military action\" is defined as the use of aerial bombs, drones, or missiles (including cruise or ballistic missiles) launched by Israeli military forces that impact Gaza ground territory.\n\nA strike on any area within the terrestrial territory of the Gaza Strip counts, including buffer zones.\n\nMissiles or drones that are intercepted and surface-to-air missile strikes will not be sufficient for a \"Yes\" resolution, regardless of whether they land on Gaza territory or cause damage.\n\nActions such as artillery fire, small arms fire, FPV or ground based ATGM strikes, ground incursions, naval shelling, cyberattacks, or other operations conducted by Israeli ground operatives will not qualify.\n\nThe primary resolution source will be official government/military statements (Israeli or foreign), multilateral bodies (UN, etc.), or a consensus of credible reporting from major international media and national broadcasters/newspapers.\n\nIf the date/time of a strike cannot be confirmed by a consensus of credible reporting by the end of the third calendar date after this market's end date, it will resolve to \"No\" regardless of whether a strike was later confirmed to have taken place.",
    "outcome": "March 29",
    "explanation": "\n# Executive Summary\nThe outcome of Israeli military action occurring on March 29 was driven by a combination of delays due to diplomatic negotiations and multi-front pressures. Initially expected around March 21, the operation was postponed as efforts to disarm Hamas and manage tensions with Iran took priority. Finally, on March 29, the strikes were launched.\n\n# Timeline Of Key Events\n- 2026-03-21: Initial expected date of operation, delayed due to diplomatic talks and ceasefire status [art_general_20260325_013_61224fea].\n- 2026-03-23: Continued humanitarian struggles and shaky ceasefire conditions [art_general_20260325_016_ab57b045].\n- 2026-03-28: Decisions made to proceed with the military operation.\n- 2026-03-29: Final outcome, military action occurs [art_general_20260325_011_afc91ebd].\n\n# Causal Chain Analysis\n- The root cause was the ongoing conflict and failure of a stable ceasefire [art_general_20260325_004_073cf632].\n- This caused diplomatic delays around March 21 to manage multiple fronts [art_general_20260325_013_61224fea].\n- These delays led to further military planning, which resulted in the final action on March 29.\n\n# Countervailing Factors\n- Ongoing diplomatic efforts, including a UN disarmament plan [art_general_20260325_017_412bb6cf], pushed against military action but ultimately failed to prevent it.\n\n# Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: March 21 Expected Operation (Delayed). Date: 2026-03-21. Causally significant as it explains the initial market expectation. Source: [art_general_20260325_013_61224fea].\n- E2: Military Action Occurs. Date: 2026-03-29. The target event. Source: [art_general_20260325_011_afc91ebd].\n\n# Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|-------------|-------------|--------------|------------------|-------|\n| March 21 expectation | art_general_20260325_013_61224fea | 2026-03-21 | 0.9 | Operation was planned |\n| Action occurs | art_general_20260325_011_afc91ebd | 2026-03-29 | 1.0 | Strikes carried out |\n\n# Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\n- If the UN disarmament plan had succeeded, the military action might have been avoided altogether or further delayed.\n",
