    "id": "q_science_20251218_002_e21fbc54",
    "title": "How many functional 'junk DNA' switches (enhancers) linked to Alzheimer's disease would researchers identify in the UNSW Sydney study published in December 2025?",
    "background": "No background available.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The number of functional enhancers will be determined by the results reported in the study from the University of New South Wales, published in Nature Neuroscience on December 18, 2025.",
    "outcome": 150,
    "explanation": "## Executive Summary\nThe identification of 150 functional 'junk DNA' switches (enhancers) linked to Alzheimer's disease was driven by a major study conducted by researchers at UNSW Sydney. Using CRISPR inhibition (CRISPRi) and single-cell RNA sequencing on human astrocytes, the team assessed nearly 1,000 potential enhancer regions. Their high-throughput approach successfully mapped out about 150 functional enhancers that regulate genes associated with Alzheimer's disease. This narrowed down the vast non-coding genome to specific regulatory elements, compiling these findings into a critical resource called AstroREG, which ultimately resolved the question at 150.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- Before December 2025: Researchers at UNSW Sydney conceptualized a study to investigate the non-coding genome (so-called \"junk DNA\") in human astrocytes, recognizing their role as support cells implicated in Alzheimer's disease [art_science_20251218_003_bfc038be].\n- During the study: The team utilized CRISPRi to temporarily silence specific DNA regions and tracked gene activity using single-cell RNA sequencing, targeting nearly 1000 candidate enhancer regions [art_science_20251218_003_bfc038be].\n- December 2025: The findings were finalized and submitted for publication, identifying that silencing certain regions altered gene expression in ways that pointed to functional enhancers [art_science_20251218_003_bfc038be].\n- December 18, 2025: The study was formally published in *Nature Neuroscience*, revealing the successful identification of 150 functional enhancers out of the 1,000 candidates tested, all linked to Alzheimer's disease [art_science_20251218_003_bfc038be].\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe outcome was **caused** by the systematic application of advanced genetic screening technologies. Specifically, the UNSW researchers recognized that standard gene-centric approaches missed the 98% of the genome consisting of non-coding DNA. This **led to** the use of CRISPRi combined with single-cell RNA sequencing to silence and track nearly 1,000 candidate regions in astrocytes [art_science_20251218_003_bfc038be]. The precision of this technique **resulted in** the successful observation that silencing certain regions altered gene expression. This rigorous filtering process **triggered** the definitive validation of about 150 functional enhancers. The formal peer-reviewed publication of these results in *Nature Neuroscience* **because** of this robust methodology ultimately fixed the ground truth at 150 [art_science_20251218_003_bfc038be].\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nThe sheer size of the non-coding genome (98% of all DNA) and the fact that enhancers can be located hundreds of thousands of DNA letters away from the genes they control posed a significant challenge. Historically, this has made enhancers extremely difficult to investigate. However, this challenge was overcome by the innovative use of CRISPRi paired with single-cell RNA sequencing, which allowed for high-throughput, simultaneous testing of nearly 1,000 candidates, preventing the study from yielding zero or very few identified enhancers.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: Initiation of astrocyte CRISPRi screening\n  - Date: Sometime before December 2025\n  - why it matters causally: Provided the methodological foundation necessary to test non-coding DNA at scale.\n  - source article IDs: [art_science_20251218_003_bfc038be]\n- E2: Identification of the 150 functional enhancers\n  - Date: Prior to December 18, 2025\n  - why it matters causally: The direct scientific discovery that answered the research question.\n  - source article IDs: [art_science_20251218_003_bfc038be]\n- E3: Publication of the study in Nature Neuroscience\n  - Date: December 18, 2025 (or shortly before)\n  - why it matters causally: Formalized the findings and established the definitive count of 150 enhancers for the scientific community and the resolution of the question.\n  - source article IDs: [art_science_20251218_003_bfc038be]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| UNSW researchers used CRISPRi on nearly 1000 candidate regions in astrocytes. | [art_science_20251218_003_bfc038be] | 2025-12-18 | 1.0 | Clear methodological description. |\n| The study identified 150 functional enhancers linked to Alzheimer's disease. | [art_science_20251218_003_bfc038be] | 2025-12-18 | 1.0 | Explicitly stated in the text. |\n| Published in Nature Neuroscience. | [art_science_20251218_003_bfc038be] | 2025-12-18 | 1.0 | Journal explicitly named. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nThere is minimal uncertainty regarding the final number, as it is explicitly stated in the publication and broadly reported. An alternative path could have occurred if the CRISPRi screening had a lower success rate, which would have resulted in fewer than 150 enhancers being identified, or if technical limitations had prevented the simultaneous screening of 1,000 candidates. However, the robustness of the combined CRISPRi and single-cell RNA sequencing methodology ensured the successful high-yield outcome.\n",
