    "title": "Khamenei out as Supreme Leader of Iran by February 28?",
    "background": "No background available.",
    "resolution_criteria": "This market will resolve to \"Yes\" if Iran's Supreme Leader, Ali Khamenei, is removed from power for any length of time between this market's creation and February 28, 2026, 11:59 PM ET. Otherwise, this market will resolve to \"No\".\n\nKhamenei will be considered to be removed from power if he resigns, is detained, or otherwise loses his position or is prevented from fulfilling his duties as Supreme Leader of Iran within this market's timeframe.\n\nThe primary resolution source for this market will be a consensus of credible reporting.",
    "outcome": "Yes",
    "explanation": "\n# Executive Summary\nThe outcome of Ali Khamenei no longer serving as Supreme Leader of Iran by February 28, 2026, was fundamentally caused by his death in a joint US-Israeli military airstrike. Escaping earlier domestic pressures from widespread economic protests, the Iranian regime faced mounting international tensions that culminated in direct military confrontation. In late February 2026, targeted strikes on Tehran destroyed Khamenei's compound, resulting in his immediate death. This sudden decapitation of the Iranian state triggered the rapid convening of the Assembly of Experts and the subsequent elevation of his son, Mojtaba Khamenei, as the new Supreme Leader.\n\n# Timeline Of Key Events\n- 2026-01-23: Iranian economic protests turned deadly amid escalating domestic unrest and violent state crackdowns [art_politics_20260123_019_70f273ba].\n- 2026-01-19 to 2026-01-30: Growing international tensions, with US President Donald Trump issuing threats and signaling a shift away from peaceful containment [art_general_20260119_002_6ee739cd].\n- 2026-02-28: A joint US-Israeli military mission launched targeted airstrikes on Tehran, successfully hitting the Supreme Leader's compound and killing Ayatollah Ali Khamenei [art_politics_20260228_003_8f202630].\n- 2026-02-28: Mojtaba Khamenei assumed rapid de facto control and issued warnings of continued conflict [art_politics_20260228_004_65eb54fd].\n- 2026-03-01: News of the successful strike broke globally, causing prediction markets to overwhelmingly favor the 'Yes' outcome as citizens woke up to the regime change [art_politics_20260301_005_e19d8925].\n- 2026-03-02: Iran's Assembly of Experts convened within days to formalize the succession process and officially select Mojtaba Khamenei [art_politics_20260302_006_1068164b].\n\n# Causal Chain Analysis\nThe causal chain leading to Khamenei's removal from power was primarily driven by external military intervention rather than internal succession plans. Root causes can be traced to escalating geopolitical tensions between Iran, Israel, and the United States, exacerbated by domestic instability from economic protests in January 2026 that weakened the regime's international standing [art_politics_20260123_019_70f273ba]. The intermediate mechanism was the decision by the US and Israel to launch a decisive military operation targeting the highest echelons of Iranian leadership. The proximate trigger was the successful airstrike on Khamenei's compound in Tehran on February 28 [art_politics_20260228_003_8f202630]. This physical destruction led directly to the final outcome: Khamenei's death and his consequent removal as Supreme Leader, forcing an immediate transition of power to his son [art_politics_20260228_004_65eb54fd].\n\n# Countervailing Factors\nThe main countervailing factor was the intense security apparatus surrounding the Supreme Leader, designed specifically to protect him from such decapitation strikes. Iranian air defenses and bunker systems were intended to withstand direct assaults. However, these defenses failed or were overwhelmed by the sophistication and scale of the joint US-Israeli military operation. Additionally, earlier market sentiment fluctuated (e.g., in late January and mid-February) due to the assumption that Khamenei would survive the domestic protests and that the US might opt for diplomatic leverage rather than a direct, highly provocative assassination strike. \n\n# Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: Escalation of Iranian economic protests resulting in deadly clashes.\n  - Date: 2026-01-23\n  - Why it matters causally: Highlighted domestic instability and increased international scrutiny.\n  - Source article IDs: art_politics_20260123_019_70f273ba\n- E2: Joint US-Israeli airstrikes on Tehran targeting the Supreme Leader.\n  - Date: 2026-02-28\n  - Why it matters causally: The direct proximate cause of Khamenei's death and removal from power.\n  - Source article IDs: art_politics_20260228_003_8f202630\n- E3: Elevation of Mojtaba Khamenei as the new Supreme Leader.\n  - Date: 2026-02-28 to 2026-03-02\n  - Why it matters causally: Confirmed the permanence of Ali Khamenei's removal and the continuation of the regime under new leadership.\n  - Source article IDs: art_politics_20260228_004_65eb54fd, art_politics_20260302_006_1068164b\n\n# Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Economic protests turn deadly | art_politics_20260123_019_70f273ba | 2026-01-23 | 0.9 | Precursor of domestic instability. |\n| US-Israel strike kills Khamenei | art_politics_20260228_003_8f202630, art_politics_20260301_005_e19d8925 | 2026-02-28 | 1.0 | Core proximate cause. |\n| Mojtaba Khamenei assumes power | art_politics_20260228_004_65eb54fd, art_politics_20260302_006_1068164b | 2026-02-28 | 0.95 | Succession confirmed. |\n\n# Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nThe primary uncertainty leading up to the resolution was whether Khamenei might step down voluntarily due to health issues or be overthrown by the mounting domestic protests. An alternative path could have seen the Iranian military or IRGC launch a preemptive strike or successfully defend the compound, allowing Khamenei to survive the February deadline. There was also uncertainty regarding how quickly the Assembly of Experts would formally ratify the succession amidst the chaos of the airstrikes.\n",
