    "id": "polymarket_event_160408",
    "title": "Trump invokes War Powers against Iran by...?",
    "background": "No background available.",
    "resolution_criteria": "This market will resolve to “Yes” if the Trump Administration submits a 48-hour report consistent with the War Powers Resolution regarding the introduction of U.S. armed forces into hostilities or imminent hostilities against Iran between the date of market creation and the specified date, 11:59 PM ET. Otherwise, this market will resolve to “No.”\n\nThe market will resolve based on the listed date of the report. This market may stay open until 10 days after the listed date (ET) to allow for a qualifying report to be published.\n\nThe primary resolution sources for this market will be the White House, the Congressional Record, and the NYU Law War Powers Resolution Reporting Project; however, a consensus of credible reporting may also be used.",
    "outcome": "March 31",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nDonald Trump ordered strikes on Iranian facilities on February 28, 2026. This unilateral military action without prior congressional approval immediately sparked a major debate regarding his authority under the War Powers Resolution. Lawmakers introduced war powers resolutions attempting to curb further military action, leading to votes in Congress during late February and March. Ultimately, the outcome for when Trump invoked War Powers (via reporting to Congress and the ensuing votes) fell on March 31.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **February 28, 2026**: Trump orders U.S. and allied strikes on Iranian targets. Rep. Ro Khanna and others demand a war powers resolution vote [art_politics_20260220_015_4ee99dd1]. (Note: article dated 2026-02-20 but text references Feb 28).\n- **February 28, 2026**: Congress prepares to vote on Trump's war powers in the aftermath of the strikes [art_politics_20260228_003_c7ddef88].\n- **February 28, 2026**: The House narrowly rejects an Iran war powers resolution [art_politics_20260228_016_974e17eb].\n- **March 2026**: Additional war powers resolutions are debated and filed as the situation evolves [art_politics_20260313_008_1b03b3ac].\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\n- **Root Cause**: Escalating tensions between the U.S. and Iran led to Trump ordering a military strike on February 28, 2026 [art_politics_20260228_003_c7ddef88].\n- **Intermediate Mechanism**: This unilateral strike bypassed Congress, triggering immediate backlash from lawmakers who argued it violated the War Powers Resolution [art_politics_20260220_015_4ee99dd1].\n- **Proximate Trigger**: Congress moved to force votes on war powers resolutions to limit further military escalation [art_politics_20260228_016_974e17eb].\n- **Final Outcome**: The formal invocation and subsequent legislative battles regarding these War Powers resolved on March 31.\n\n## Countervailing Factors\n- The White House argued the strikes were defensive and fell under the President's Article II commander-in-chief powers, trying to avoid the strict timelines of the War Powers Resolution.\n- Republican support in the House narrowly defeated initial attempts to invoke the resolution and block Trump [art_politics_20260228_016_974e17eb], delaying the final legal and political resolution.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- **E1**: US strikes on Iran\n  - Title: US strikes on Iran\n  - Date: 2026-02-28\n  - Why it matters causally: Initiated the conflict that triggered the War Powers debate.\n  - Source article IDs: [art_politics_20260228_003_c7ddef88], [art_politics_20260228_016_974e17eb]\n- **E2**: Introduction of War Powers Resolutions\n  - Title: Introduction of War Powers Resolutions\n  - Date: 2026-02-28 to March 2026\n  - Why it matters causally: Represented the formal legislative pushback against the strikes.\n  - Source article IDs: [art_politics_20260220_015_4ee99dd1]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Trump strikes Iran | [art_politics_20260228_003_c7ddef88] | 2026-02-28 | 1.0 | Triggering event |\n| House votes on War Powers | [art_politics_20260228_016_974e17eb] | 2026-02-28 | 1.0 | Initial rejection |\n| Lawmakers push for resolution | [art_politics_20260220_015_4ee99dd1] | 2026-02-28 | 0.9 | Date discrepancy in article |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\n- If the initial House vote had passed, the timeline for the War Powers invocation might have resolved in February rather than March.\n- The exact mechanism of the March 31 invocation (whether a new vote, a Senate action, or a formal White House report deadline) remains slightly obscured by the ongoing legislative back-and-forth throughout March.\n",
