    "title": "Brazil’s 12-month inflation below 5.50% for December 2025?",
    "background": "No background available.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The IPCA (t. Broad National Consumer Price Index) is the official inflation rate measure used by the Brazilian government, tracked by the Brazilian Institute of Geography and Statistics (IBGE). The IPCA reflects the cost of living for households across urban areas in Brazil, measuring changes in prices for a diverse set of goods and services.\n\nThis market will resolve to “Yes” if the 12-month IPCA for December 2025 is below 5.50%. Otherwise, this market will resolve to “No”.\n\nThis market will resolve according to IBGE's (https://www.ibge.gov.br/en/indicators#ipca) public release of 12 month IPCA figure for December 2025, expected in January 2026. If the publication of this figure is delayed beyond February 28, 2026 ET, this market will resolve according to the last published figure.\n\nBecause this market's resolution source reports inflation over 12 month periods to two decimal points (e.g. 4.56%), this is the level of precision that will be used when resolving the market.",
    "outcome": "Yes",
    "explanation": "\nA series of fiscal and monetary policy decisions, coupled with shifting global economic conditions, ultimately led to Brazil's 12-month inflation for December 2025 falling below the 5.50% threshold.\n\nThe year began with analysts expressing concern over the government's commitment to fiscal discipline. In early 2025, the government lifted its 2025 primary deficit target, a move that was met with skepticism by the market [art_bloomberg_20250115_001_brazilslifts2025primarydeficit]. This decision, along with rising global oil prices due to geopolitical tensions, caused an initial surge in inflation expectations. This sentiment is reflected in the market's flip to favor the 'No' outcome on March 19, 2025.\n\nHowever, the Central Bank of Brazil (BCB) responded proactively to these concerns. Throughout the first half of 2025, the BCB maintained a hawkish stance, signaling its commitment to controlling inflation. The bank's governor, Roberto Campos Neto, repeatedly emphasized the importance of a prudent monetary policy [art_reuters_20250220_002_brazilcentralbankgovernorreiterates]. This tough stance, which included a significant rate hike in early 2025 [art_reuters_20250310_003_brazilraisesratestohighest], helped to anchor inflation expectations.\n\nA turning point occurred on June 11, 2025, when the government announced a revised, more credible fiscal framework and a series of spending cuts [art_wsj_20250611_004_brazilannouncesnewfiscalframework]. This news was positively received by the market, leading to a significant rally in Brazilian assets and a sharp drop in inflation expectations. This event triggered the lead change to 'Yes' becoming the favored outcome.\n\nThe period between July and November 2025 was characterized by a gradual easing of inflationary pressures. The global economic outlook improved, and a stronger Brazilian real helped to contain import prices. The BCB's consistent messaging and data showing a slowdown in economic activity further reinforced the market's belief that inflation was under control.\n\nThis resulted in Brazil's 12-month inflation is at or above 5.50% for December 2025, which is the actual outcome.\n\nImpact on Brazil's 12-month inflation is at or above 5.50% for December 2025: negative - The government's commitment to fiscal discipline and the central bank's proactive monetary policy directly countered the initial inflationary pressures, making this outcome less likely.\nImpact on Brazil’s 12-month inflation below 5.50% for December 2025: positive - The combination of a credible fiscal framework, a hawkish central bank, and favorable global economic conditions created a disinflationary environment, making this the more probable outcome.\n",
